ToyOS — third-party material
============================

ToyOS is licensed under MIT OR Apache-2.0, at your option. Both texts are in
the repository: `LICENSE-MIT` and `LICENSE-APACHE`.

Some of what this repository carries is not ours and is under other terms.
This file is the list. It exists because until 2026-08-08 there wasn't one:
`specs/assessments/dependency-audit-2026-08-08.md` §7 found six committed binary files —
11,094,369 bytes of the 14,634,080 git tracks — plus a source corpus, all
outside the declared licence and none of them documented. Nothing here is a
violation. Everything here is an obligation somebody has to be able to find.

Provenance below was established by inspecting content, not by trusting
memory: `assets/` and `ovmf/` both entered in the initial squashed commit and
carry no attribution of their own. Every hash is `shasum -a 256`, taken
2026-08-08.

**If you redistribute a ToyOS image, read the DOOM1.WAD entry.** It is the one
that constrains what you may do with a build.


assets/DOOM1.WAD — id Software, Doom shareware IWAD
---------------------------------------------------

    4,196,020 bytes, `IWAD` magic
    sha256 1d7d43be501e67d927e415e0b8f3e29c3bf33075e859721816f652a526cac771
    Copyright (c) 1994 Id Software
    Terms: licenses/id-software-shareware-DOOM1.txt

id permits free redistribution of this file. John Carmack, 1999: *"The DOOM
shareware wad is freely distributable."* Two conditions come with it, both from
the Limited Use Software License Agreement carried beside it:

- **Unmodified.** §1 forbids modifying the Software or creating derivative
  works from it. The file here is byte-for-byte the shareware IWAD.
- **Not for consideration.** §2 permits making copies "to give to other
  persons" and forbids charging or receiving anything for them without id's
  prior written consent.

**So a ToyOS image that carries this file may not be sold.** That is the whole
of the practical constraint, and it is the reason this entry leads.

It ships in the initrd of the default image (`system.toml`) and of
`--console-boot`; `--diag-boot` declares no assets and carries none.

**This entry is meant to become deletable.** The owner's position, 2026-08-08:
doom is a milestone demonstration and not a required part of ToyOS, and the
intended end state is a package manager fetching it. When the WAD stops being
committed, delete this section rather than softening it. An asset that is not
in the working tree is named by `src/assets.rs` and skipped, so an image built
without one says so instead of quietly going without.


assets/soundfont.sf2 — GeneralUser GS, "License v2.0"
------------------------------------------------------

    15,546,764 bytes, `RIFF`/`sfbk`
    sha256 89a13a5c907b5cc83c15679e07e6dcb06fd72102937e092dc4a582f1aa5905c3
    Copyright 1997-2025 by S. Christian Collins
    Licence text: licenses/GeneralUser-GS-License-v2.0.txt

doom's music, and a **derivative**: `src/soundfont.rs` cuts GeneralUser GS
v2.0.3 down to the 37 melodic programs and 23 percussion keys
`assets/DOOM1.WAD`'s own MUS headers select, and
`cargo run -- --regen-soundfont <bank.sf2>` re-runs that on the whole bank
(32,319,396 bytes,
sha256 9575028c7a1f589f5770fccc8cff2734566af40cd26ed836944e9a5152688cfe, from
https://www.schristiancollins.com — not committed, because shipping both is
shipping the same samples twice). Every one of the source's `INFO` chunks
travels into the output unread, so the copyright above and the whole licence
text are **inside the shipped file** as well as beside it —
`the_shipped_bank_still_carries_its_source_licence` is that as a test, and the
text in `licenses/` was read back out of the file.

**It is not CC-BY-4.0.** `system.toml` said so until the author's own words were
read; the record is corrected here. It is a bespoke "License v2.0" by
S. Christian Collins, permissive in substance — "use GeneralUser GS without
restriction for your own music creation, private or commercial", "feel free to
use it in your software projects, and to modify the SoundFont bank or its
packaging" — but not a standard licence, with no patent grant and no warranty
clause.

**The caveat is the author's own and belongs in plain sight.** He writes that
GeneralUser GS began as a personal project never meant for publication, and:

> I cannot be 100% sure where all of the samples originated, although I do know
> that none of them came from commercially published SoundFont packages or
> sample CDs.

He asks anyone who becomes aware of a restricted sample to tell him so he can
replace it, and records that he has had no ownership complaint since 2000. The
owner took this file with that in front of him, 2026-08-08. The licence also
asks that others not link directly to his download files — a request about how
to obtain it rather than a restriction on redistributing it, and this repository
carries its own copy rather than fetching one.

**This entry is meant to become deletable**, exactly as the `DOOM1.WAD` entry
is. `specs/doom-music-soundfont.md` §5 records the intended exit: an OPL3
synthesiser driven by the `GENMIDI` lump already inside `DOOM1.WAD`, which adds
zero bytes and raises no licence question at all, priced at 3–6 days. Taking it
deletes this file and this section — though not the 15,546,764 bytes already in
git history, which is the part of this decision that cannot be undone. When that
happens, delete this section rather than softening it.


userland/doom — GPL-2.0
-----------------------

    Licence text: licenses/GPL-2.0-doomgeneric.txt

`/bin/doom` links doomgeneric, so the program is GPL-2.0. The C sources are not
committed: `userland/doom/build.rs` fetches upstream commit `fc60163` into a
gitignored directory and `toyos-cc` compiles it. `forks.toml` `[doomgeneric]`
records the pin and why it is a pin.

The licence text is carried here because the fetch does not bring it: the
extraction takes only the archive's `doomgeneric/` subdirectory, and upstream's
`LICENSE` sits at the repository root. It is that file, verbatim.

Every source file in that tree carries "Copyright(C) 1993-1996 Id Software,
Inc." and "Copyright(C) 2005-2014 Simon Howard" over GPL "version 2 ... or any
later version" — id's Doom source release by way of Chocolate Doom. The crate
declares `GPL-2.0-only`, which is a narrowing a distributor may make.

Nothing else in this repository is GPL, and no GPL code is linked into the
kernel, the SDK, or any other program.


assets/JetBrainsMono-Regular.ttf — SIL Open Font License 1.1
------------------------------------------------------------

    270,224 bytes, version 2.305
    sha256 e6fd0d7e91550b3ed2b735d4312474362c4716edc4fc0577a0f61ed782d5aed1
    Copyright 2020 The JetBrains Mono Project Authors
    (https://github.com/JetBrains/JetBrainsMono)
    Licence text: licenses/OFL-1.1-JetBrainsMono.txt

Read out of the font's own name table, which states the copyright and the
licence verbatim. The OFL requires both to travel with the Font Software; until
this file neither did.

Two derived artifacts come from it, and whether a 1-bit rasterization is still
"the Font Software" is a real question nobody here has answered:

- `kernel/src/drivers/panic_console/font8x16.bin` (1,520 bytes, committed,
  sha256 e1bea9791e07a0e2509196c6cb4563d44cafca1f19b0ef660319b0fa53546a3e),
  regenerated by `cargo run -- --regen-font`.
- `share/fonts/JetBrainsMono-Regular-8x16.font` in every initrd that ships
  `assets/`, rasterized at build time by `src/assets.rs`.

The OFL's reserved-name clause is not engaged: neither artifact is distributed
as a font under the JetBrains Mono name.


