ovmf/*.fd — EDK II firmware, BSD-2-Clause-Patent
------------------------------------------------

    OVMF_CODE-pure-efi.fd  1,966,080 bytes
      sha256 9de33971d47958f42af86584b502f83256120b2482e4f7ed14db32fd68e92922
    OVMF_VARS-pure-efi.fd    131,072 bytes
      sha256 c653de93db67e4f2213a35598efb379a13ef4a12c241e003699d4d7afd193635
    DEBUGX64_OVMF.fd       4,194,304 bytes
      sha256 800ff5af1220d1232d4da7173ccddbb74a9217600bd8935903d9d534801778b4

    Copyright (c) 2019, TianoCore and contributors
    Licence text: licenses/BSD-2-Clause-Patent-EDK2.txt

Third-party builds of Tianocore EDK II, read out of the binaries' own build
paths: the two `-pure-efi` files were built on a Jenkins worker from
`edk2-gf0064ac3af`, and `DEBUGX64_OVMF.fd` from a different tree (`/src/edk2`,
`DEBUG_GCC5`). No `LICENSE`, version record or build recipe came with them.

`OVMF_CODE`/`OVMF_VARS` are load-bearing — `src/qemu.rs:101` and
`tests/common/qemu.rs:2207` point QEMU's pflash at them, so every boot on the
dev host uses them. **`DEBUGX64_OVMF.fd` is referenced by nothing**: it is
4,194,304 bytes of firmware no code path reaches. The audit missed it, calling
all three load-bearing; whether to keep it is the owner's.

Two open questions for the owner, both recorded in
`specs/assessments/dependency-audit-2026-08-08.md` §7e: whether to record a real upstream
and version for these, and whether to take the firmware from QEMU's own
installation instead — which would put it inside the "comes with QEMU"
allowance and delete 6,291,456 bytes from the repository.


tests/testcases/ — TinyCC's corpus, LGPL-2.1, and picoc, BSD-3-Clause
---------------------------------------------------------------------

The C cases `toyos-cc` is tested against are mostly TinyCC's `tests/tests2` and
`tests/pp`. They are compiler *input* — data, not linked code — so their terms
do not reach this repository's own code, but they must still be attributed.
`tests/testcases/LICENSE` is the attribution, and it says file by file what is
whose, with the counts it was established from.

One case was deleted on 2026-08-08 rather than attributed:
`tests/testcases/tinycc/46_grep.c`, "Copyright (C) 1980, DECUS", granting
permission to copy or modify "but not for profit". That is not a free licence
and it was never TinyCC's to relicense. Do not re-import it.


Rust crates and the forks
-------------------------

Third-party crates keep their own upstream licences; nothing is vendored.
`forks.toml` is the manifest of every crate ToyOS patches, with each fork's
upstream, pinned base and licence. The audit read all fourteen fork manifests
and found them permissive throughout: Apache-2.0, MIT, or MIT OR Apache-2.0.

`rust/` is a submodule of a fork of the Rust compiler and standard library, and
carries upstream's own licensing.
